    <article class="help-body">
        <h1>DistillForge help</h1>
        <p class="lead">
            DistillForge reads a document (a meeting transcript, a plan, an email thread), uses an AI model to pull out the action points,
            and shows them as sticky notes you can sort into categories. Every note links back to the passage it came from.
        </p>

        <section id="start">
            <h2>Getting started</h2>
            <ol>
                <li><strong>Set up an AI model</strong> (once). Click <kbd>⚙</kbd> in the top bar and add a provider and a model. See <a href="help#ai">Setting up AI</a>.</li>
                <li>Click <strong>Add Source</strong> (or <strong>+ New</strong>), paste your text, choose the document type if you know it, and pick the AI model to use.</li>
                <li>Click <strong>Add and extract</strong>. A progress window shows the work; you can <em>Hide</em> it or <em>Cancel extraction</em>.</li>
                <li>New notes appear in the <strong>Uncategorised</strong> panel. Click a note to see where it came from in the source text on the left.</li>
                <li>Create categories along the bottom and drag notes into them.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section id="how">
            <h2>How extraction works</h2>
            <p>
                AI models can only read a limited amount of text at once (their <em>context window</em>). DistillForge splits your document
                into passages and sends them in <strong>windows</strong> that fit. Neighbouring windows overlap slightly so a task that
                straddles a boundary isn't missed.
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Pass 1 · Extracting</strong>: the model reads each window and returns action points, each with a short quote from the source.
                    The app finds those quotes in your document itself, so the highlight is exact and the model can't invent locations.</li>
                <li><strong>Pass 2 · Consolidating</strong>: when there was more than one window, the model is asked which notes describe the same task.
                    Clear repeats are merged. It can only suggest merges; the app does the merging, so evidence is never lost or rewritten.
                    Notes that <em>might</em> be the same task are not merged but flagged for you (see <a href="help#duplicates">Duplicates and merging</a>).</li>
                <li>A window that fails is skipped with a warning rather than failing the whole run. Hover the status line above the notes to see warnings.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                <strong>Cancel extraction</strong> stops the run within a few seconds. Your existing notes are not changed: new notes are only saved when a run finishes.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section id="notes">
            <h2>Working with notes</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Click a note</strong> to highlight its evidence in the source (click again to clear). A <span class="chip">low confidence</span> tag means the
                    model's quote couldn't be matched exactly, so the highlight covers the whole passage.</li>
                <li><strong>⤢</strong> on a note opens it for editing: title, summary, owner, due date and colour. The source evidence is shown read-only.</li>
                <li><strong>Colours:</strong> by default notes are pink, or yellow when they have a deadline. Choose any colour to override this; choosing the default colour goes back to automatic.</li>
                <li><strong>Filters</strong> above the Uncategorised notes narrow the list by <em>Party</em> (owner), <em>Deadline</em> and <em>Duplicates</em> (only notes flagged ⚠ as possible duplicates). They only affect the Uncategorised panel.</li>
                <li>A <strong>⚠</strong> before a title and a <span class="chip">+2 merged</span> tag mean the note has possible duplicates or other notes merged into it; see <a href="help#duplicates">Duplicates and merging</a>.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="duplicates">
            <h2>Duplicates and merging</h2>
            <p>
                The same task is sometimes raised more than once: a request near the start of a meeting and a confirmation at the close, for example.
                These can be real, separate notes or two mentions of one task, so DistillForge flags them and lets you decide.
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>A <strong>⚠</strong> before a note's title means it is a <em>possible duplicate</em> of another note. Use the <em>Duplicates</em> filter to show only those.</li>
                <li>Open the note (<strong>⤢</strong>) to see the notes it might duplicate, with their owner, due date and category. For each one you can
                    <strong>Merge it into this note</strong>, <strong>Merge this into it</strong>, or say <strong>Not a duplicate</strong> to clear the flag.</li>
                <li><strong>Drag a note onto another note</strong> to merge it into that one (you'll be asked to confirm).</li>
                <li>A merged note is hidden from the main view and follows the note it was merged into, including its category. The note that absorbed it shows a
                    <span class="chip">+1 merged</span> tag, and clicking it highlights the source passages of all of them.</li>
                <li>Nothing is lost. A merged note keeps its own text and evidence, and <strong>Unmerge</strong> in the parent's details brings it back as a separate note.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                <strong>Treat the ⚠ as a suggestion.</strong> The flags come from the AI and a simple title comparison, so some point at notes that are only on a related topic.
                If a flag is wrong, open the note and choose <em>Not a duplicate</em>. A flag can also remain on a note after you merge it with one duplicate, if it was also flagged
                against a different note; open the note to see which.
            </p>
            <p class="note-callout">Merging into a note that has already absorbed others keeps everything in one group; a note can only be merged one level deep.
                Flags appear on notes from a new extraction. Re-extract an older workspace to get them.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="categories">
            <h2>Categories</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>＋</strong> adds a category (name and an optional accent colour). Names must be unique within a workspace.</li>
                <li><strong>✎</strong> (or double-click a tab) renames or recolours the selected category. <strong>✕</strong> deletes it; its notes go back to Uncategorised.</li>
                <li><strong>Drag and drop:</strong> drag a note from Uncategorised onto a category tab, or into the category area. Drag it back onto the Uncategorised panel to uncategorise it.
                    Dragging needs a mouse or trackpad; touch screens aren't supported yet.</li>
                <li>Notes keep their own colour inside a category; the category's colour only accents its tab.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="note-callout">Re-extracting a document replaces its extracted notes, including any you've edited or filed into categories. Categories themselves are kept.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="workspaces">
            <h2>Workspaces</h2>
            <p>Each document lives in its own workspace, with its own notes and categories.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Switch with the dropdown above the source; <strong>+ New</strong> starts another.</li>
                <li><strong>Replace Source</strong> swaps the document and clears everything derived from it (notes, categories, previous results).</li>
                <li><strong>Re-extract</strong> runs the AI again on the same text, optionally with a different model, and asks first because it replaces the extracted notes.</li>
                <li><strong>Archive</strong> hides a workspace and makes it read-only. Tick <em>Show archived</em> to see archived ones; <strong>Unarchive</strong> restores editing.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="ai">
            <h2>Setting up AI</h2>
            <p>
                DistillForge works with any service that speaks the OpenAI chat-completions API: local servers such as <strong>Ollama</strong> or <strong>LM Studio</strong>,
                or hosted ones such as <strong>OpenAI</strong> and <strong>OpenRouter</strong>. Click <kbd>⚙</kbd> to manage them.
            </p>
            <h3>Providers</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Base URL</strong> must include the version path, usually <code>/v1</code> (for example <code>http://localhost:11434/v1</code>).</li>
                <li><strong>API key</strong>: leave blank for local servers that don't need one. Keys are encrypted on the server and never shown again; use <em>Replace</em> or <em>Remove</em> to change one.</li>
                <li><strong>Test connection</strong> checks the URL and key and lists the models the server offers.</li>
                <li>Presets fill in the URL for common services.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Models</h3>
            <p>A provider can have several models. For each one you set:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Model ID</strong>: pick from the list or type it exactly as the service names it (for example <code>llama3.1:8b</code>).</li>
                <li><strong>Context window</strong>: how many tokens the model is actually run with. This can't be discovered automatically. For Ollama, use the <code>num_ctx</code> it runs with, not the model's advertised maximum.</li>
                <li><strong>Reserved output</strong>: tokens kept free for the model's answer. It must be smaller than the context window.</li>
                <li><strong>Structured output</strong>: <em>JSON schema</em> is the most reliable. If a service rejects it, try <em>JSON object</em>, then <em>None</em>.</li>
                <li><strong>Default</strong>: the model used when you don't choose one. The first model you add becomes the default.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Choose the model for each extraction from the dropdown next to <em>Re-extract</em> or in the Add Source window. A workspace remembers the model it last used.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="tuning">
            <h2>Getting better results</h2>
            <p>
                The <strong>context window</strong> and <strong>reserved output</strong> settings do more than tell the app what the model can handle: they decide
                <em>how big each window is</em>, and window size affects the quality of what comes back.
            </p>
            <p class="formula">Space for document text in each window ≈ (context window − reserved output) × 90%, less the instructions sent with it.</p>

            <h3>Smaller windows often find more</h3>
            <p>
                A model tends to return a limited number of items per answer, however long the text. Giving it a long document in one go can mean it picks out only the most
                prominent points. Splitting the same document into more, smaller windows gives it a closer read of each part, and usually surfaces more of the quieter commitments.
                The trade-offs:
            </p>
            <table class="help-table">
                <thead><tr><th></th><th>Larger windows</th><th>Smaller windows</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><th>Notes found</th><td>Fewer, higher-level</td><td>More, more detailed</td></tr>
                    <tr><th>Risk</th><td>Missed items</td><td>Fragments of one task, or near-duplicates</td></tr>
                    <tr><th>Speed and cost</th><td>Fewer model calls</td><td>More calls, slower, more tokens</td></tr>
                    <tr><th>Duplicates</th><td>Little to merge</td><td>Relies on the consolidation pass</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h3>Try it yourself</h3>
            <ol>
                <li>Extract with your model's normal settings and note how many notes you get.</li>
                <li>Open <kbd>⚙</kbd>, edit the model, and lower the context window (a good first experiment for a local 8B model is <strong>4096</strong> with <strong>2048</strong> reserved).</li>
                <li>Click <strong>Re-extract</strong> and compare. Judge by whether the extra notes are real, separate tasks, not just by how many there are.</li>
                <li>Keep the setting that gives you the most useful notes without lots of fragments or repeats.</li>
            </ol>
            <p><strong>Tips</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Don't go too small: very small windows chop up context and can produce fragments. Windows of a few thousand tokens are usually a sensible floor.</li>
                <li>Check the status line for <em>"Consolidation skipped"</em>. With many notes and a small window, the list can be too big to send for the duplicate check, so scan the results for repeats yourself.</li>
                <li>Never set the context window higher than the server really runs the model with; text beyond it is silently cut off. The app warns when reported prompt size looks far below what it sent.</li>
                <li>Different models behave differently. It's worth trying the same document on two or three, using the model dropdown.</li>
                <li>Telling the document type apart (<em>Transcript</em> or <em>Prose</em>) when you add it can help the app split the text sensibly. Auto-detect is usually right.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="trouble">
            <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
            <dl>
                <dt>"Could not reach the API"</dt>
                <dd>The web app can't contact the server. Check the API is running and reload the page.</dd>

                <dt>"No AI model is configured"</dt>
                <dd>Add a provider and a model under <kbd>⚙</kbd>, or ask whoever runs your server to set the default AI settings.</dd>

                <dt>Test connection fails</dt>
                <dd>Check the base URL ends in <code>/v1</code> (or your service's version path), that the server is reachable from the machine running the API (not just your browser), and the API key is right.
                    Some services don't list their models; you can still type the model ID by hand.</dd>

                <dt>Extraction failed, or "interrupted"</dt>
                <dd>Read the message in the status line. "Interrupted" means the server stopped the job (for example a very long run timed out). Try a faster model or larger windows, or split the document.</dd>

                <dt>"Response was truncated"</dt>
                <dd>The model ran out of output space. Raise <em>Reserved output</em> (keeping it below the context window) or use smaller windows.</dd>

                <dt>Too few notes, or missing obvious ones</dt>
                <dd>Try smaller windows (see above), a stronger model, or check for failed windows in the warnings.</dd>

                <dt>Duplicate or near-identical notes</dt>
                <dd>Common with very small windows, especially if consolidation was skipped (see the status line). Look for the ⚠ flag and merge them (see
                    <a href="help#duplicates">Duplicates and merging</a>), or re-extract with slightly larger windows.</dd>

                <dt>A note's highlight is a whole passage, not a phrase</dt>
                <dd>That's the <span class="chip">low confidence</span> tag: the model's quote couldn't be matched exactly, so the whole segment is shown.</dd>

                <dt>Saving an API key fails</dt>
                <dd>The server needs an encryption key configured (<code>Encryption__Key</code>). Providers that need no key (such as local Ollama) work without it.</dd>
            </dl>
        </section>

        <section id="privacy">
            <h2>Your data</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>The text you add is sent to the AI provider <em>you</em> configure for extraction. If that's a hosted service, your text leaves your network; use a local model for sensitive documents.</li>
                <li>Documents, notes and categories are stored in the DistillForge database. API keys are stored encrypted and are never sent back to the browser.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
    </article>
</div>
